%I A392384 #11 Oct 12 2026 14:31:22
%S A392384 184,459,1375,6655,14739,24334
%N A392384 Numbers k such that sigma(k) = psi(k) + tau(k) + omega(k)^6.
%C A392384 The sequence is complete. Put L := Omega(k). For every nonsquarefree k with L >= 5, one has sigma(k) - psi(k) - tau(k) >= 2^(L-1) - L - 2. Since solutions require this difference to equal omega(k)^6 <= L^6, we obtain L <= 30. Exhaustive checking of the remaining prime-exponent patterns shows that solutions must have the form k = p^3*q, where p and q are distinct primes satisfying (p+1)*(q+1) = 72. This gives exactly the six listed terms. - _Max Alekseyev_, Oct 11 2026
%e A392384 184 is a term since sigma(184) = 360 = 288 + 8 + 2^6 = psi(184) + tau(184) + omega(184)^6.
%o A392384 (PARI) isok(k) = {my(f = factor(k)); sigma(f) == prod(i=1, #f~, (f[i, 1]+1) * f[i, 1]^(f[i, 2]-1)) + numdiv(f) + omega(f)^6;} \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Jan 09 2026
